# What shall we do - zeemanslied (shanty), Drunken Sailor
# Traditionele shanty van onbekende herkomst; de oudste vermelding is een verslag van een
# walvisvaart uit 1839 (Wikipedia (en), "Drunken Sailor"). Publiek domein.
# Overgenomen uit WHATSHAL.WED (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991). Titel in de bron "What shallwe do"; tikfouten
# hersteld: "shallwe" werd "shall we", "That's we'll do" werd "That's what we'll do".
# De bron noemt het refrein na het eerste couplet; het is hier na elk couplet herhaald.
{title: What shall we do}
{subtitle: Drunken Sailor}
{meta: id what-shall-we-do}
{meta: language en}
{lyricist: traditioneel}
{composer: traditioneel}
{copyright: Public domain}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: source Overgenomen uit WHATSHAL.WED (WordPerfect 5.1, 1991); herkomst via Wikipedia (en)}
{meta: tag kampvuur}
{meta: tag volkslied}

{start_of_verse}
What shall we do with a drunken sailor,
what shall we do with a drunken sailor,
what shall we do with a drunken sailor,
early in the morning
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Hoo ray and up she rises,
Hoo ray and up she rises,
Hoo ray and up she rises,
early in the morning.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Put him in the scuppers with a hose-pipe on him (3x)
early in the morning.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Put him in the longboat until he's sober (3x)
early in the morning.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Tie him by the legs in a running bowline (3x)
early in the morning.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
Pull out the plug and wet him all over (3x)
early in the morning.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse}
That's what we'll do with a drunken sailor (3x)
early in the morning.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
